# One origin, two surfaces (ADR-0010, ADR-0011).
#
# The split has to be invisible from the outside: a citation URL 307s to /app or
# /api/v1, and a redirect that crossed origins would break the Accept-based
# routing, cookies, and anything a browser scopes to a host. So a proxy owns the
# port, and the two services behind it never need to know about each other.
#
# This is also what makes the surfaces independently deployable, which was the
# point of separating them: /app can be rebuilt, rolled back, or replaced with a
# static export without the API restarting, and vice versa.
#
# $SITE_ADDRESS is the site address. It defaults to :8000, which is the dev
# stack: plain HTTP, no host matching. On the box it is
# `http://uscode.linkedlegislation.org:8000`: plain HTTP on 8000 inside the
# container, matched on the hostname, behind the shared edge Caddy that
# publishes 80 and 443 and terminates TLS for this hostname and
# statutes.linkedlegislation.org (docs/deploy.md §9, ADR-0020 as amended). A
# bare hostname is the shape before the edge: Caddy provisions and renews the
# certificate itself, on 80 and 443.

{
	# Who may name the client. `{client_ip}` in the handle blocks below is the
	# forwarded X-Forwarded-For address when the peer is in this range and the
	# peer itself otherwise. The range is the `edge` network the statutes
	# repository's deploy/edge/up.sh creates, so behind the edge the address
	# it forwards survives this hop, and on the dev stack a workstation peer
	# (192.168.65.1 on Docker Desktop, a bridge gateway on Linux) is not
	# trusted and its X-Forwarded-For is dropped. Not `private_ranges`: a
	# workstation peer on the dev stack is in a private range too and would be
	# trusted to name its own client (ADR-0029).
	#
	# X-Forwarded-Proto follows the same rule. Behind the edge the scheme is
	# https at the edge and http on this hop; the edge sends
	# X-Forwarded-Proto: https, this hop preserves it from the trusted peer,
	# and uvicorn (--proxy-headers) reads it as `request.url.scheme`. The
	# session cookie's `Secure` flag does not read it in production:
	# USC_COOKIE_SECURE=true in docker-compose.prod.yml decides that on its
	# own (api/auth.py, ADR-0019).
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static 10.83.0.0/24
	}
}

{$SITE_ADDRESS::8000} {
	encode gzip zstd

	# `header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}` in both handle blocks below
	# *overwrites* the header rather than appending to it. Both compose files
	# start uvicorn with `--forwarded-allow-ips "*"`, and in that mode its
	# proxy-headers middleware returns `x_forwarded_for_hosts[0]`, the
	# leftmost value; Astro's Node adapter reads the leftmost value too
	# (`astro/dist/core/app/node.js:121`). So the value written here is the
	# value both backends key their rate limiters on (ADR-0029).
	#
	# `{client_ip}` is the forwarded address when the peer is in the
	# `trusted_proxies` range above and the peer itself otherwise. Behind the
	# edge the peer is the edge, which has already overwritten the header with
	# the real client (its own `header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}`), so
	# the client's address survives this hop and nothing the client sent does.
	# On the dev stack the peer is the workstation, outside the range, so its
	# header is dropped and the peer is written. One line serves both shapes.
	#
	# Measured (docs/verification/xff.md): from a peer on 10.83.0.0/24 sending
	# `X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.9`, `client_ip` is 203.0.113.9; from a peer
	# on another Docker network sending the same header, `client_ip` is the
	# peer.
	# robots.txt is a property of the *host*, not of either surface, and this
	# file is the only thing that owns the host — so serving it here is what
	# makes one answer true for /app and /api/v1 alike, including for a request
	# that arrives while one of them is restarting.
	#
	# It exists because the site was measured being crawled into the ground.
	# One hour of the proxy log on 2026-08-03: 43,068 requests, of which 33,937
	# were ClaudeBot and 9,079 GPTBot — 99.9% — against roughly 48 from human
	# browsers. 36,465 of them (85%) carried `?release=`, which is the tell:
	# the crawlers had found the version dimension, and the space behind it is
	# 65,938 sections x 382 release points, about 25 million reader pages,
	# before counting the 96,185,732 `?id=` guids that also resolve. There is no
	# crawl budget that finishes that, so the load has no natural end — the box
	# sat at load average 2.06 on 2 vCPUs with nobody reading it, which is what
	# kept `uscode-cpu-credits-low` in alarm and, on a t4g in unlimited mode,
	# what was being paid for.
	#
	# The site had no robots.txt at all: five requests for one in that hour, all
	# 404. So this is not a tightening of a policy, it is the first statement of
	# one, and it is deliberately the blunt version while the demo is being
	# shown to the audience it was built for. `Disallow: /` is advisory and only
	# binds well-behaved crawlers — which is exactly what the measured traffic
	# is, both bots declaring themselves and both honouring robots.txt.
	# Shaping the crawl instead (index the ~66k current-text sections, disallow
	# the permutation space) is the setting to come back to when the site should
	# be discoverable; it is a change to this block and nothing else.
	handle /robots.txt {
		header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
		respond <<ROBOTS
			User-agent: *
			Disallow: /
			ROBOTS 200
	}

	# robots.txt is advisory, and on 2026-08-19 that stopped being enough
	# (ADR-0073). Meta's `meta-externalagent` fetched /robots.txt 21 times in 24
	# hours, read `Disallow: /`, and crawled anyway: 7,155 of the 7,172 requests
	# in one hour, spread over ~60 addresses in 57.141.0.0/24 so that no single
	# one looks like a flood. It had found the `?release=` axis, which is the same
	# ~25-million-page permutation space ADR-0037 measured ClaudeBot and GPTBot
	# walking — the difference being that those two stopped when asked.
	#
	# So this is the enforcement of a policy already stated rather than a new one:
	# the robots.txt above says `Disallow: /` to every agent, and this block is
	# what that sentence means for an agent that does not honour it. A declared
	# crawler gets 403 here, at the proxy, without reaching either backend or the
	# database — which is the property that matters, since what took the site down
	# was not bandwidth but fifteen Postgres connections held open by requests
	# nobody was waiting for.
	#
	# Matching is on the User-Agent because that is what a crawler volunteers. It
	# is trivially forgeable, and forging it is not defended against here: an
	# agent that lies about being a crawler is indistinguishable from a reader,
	# and the timeouts and pool bounds behind this (ADR-0073) are what hold in
	# that case. This list catches the honest ones, which — measured twice now —
	# is all of the traffic that has actually knocked this site over.
	#
	# The generic markers are second, and deliberately not first: `bot`, `crawler`
	# and `spider` in a User-Agent catch the next crawler without an edit here,
	# and the named list is what makes the common cases greppable. Neither
	# matches curl, uvicorn's healthcheck, or a script written against /api/v1 —
	# programmatic use of the API is the point of the API, and only self-declared
	# crawling is refused.
	@crawlers header_regexp User-Agent "(?i)(meta-externalagent|meta-webindexer|facebookexternalhit|claudebot|claude-web|gptbot|oai-searchbot|chatgpt-user|perplexitybot|perplexity-user|google-extended|googleother|applebot|amazonbot|bytespider|ccbot|cohere-ai|diffbot|imagesift|omgili|dataforseo|semrush|ahrefs|mj12|dotbot|petalbot|yandex|timpibot|webzio|scrapy|bot[/ ]|[ /(]bot\b|crawler|spider)"
	handle @crawlers {
		header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
		respond "This site is closed to automated crawling. See /robots.txt." 403
	}

	# The third measurement, 2026-09-19 (ADR-0088), and the first one neither
	# robots.txt nor the block above can touch. One hour of this proxy's log:
	# 15,307 requests from **11,835 distinct addresses** — 1.3 requests each,
	# across 3,758 /16s — every one of them presenting a plausible Chrome
	# User-Agent, 84% of them carrying `?release=` or `?date=`. Nothing declares
	# itself, so the crawler list matches none of it; nothing repeats an address,
	# so ADR-0029's per-caller budgets never see a second request from the same
	# key. It is one scrape of the ~25-million-page permutation space behind a
	# rotating address pool, and it is what emptied the box's CPU credits and put
	# the reader into hourly 503s.
	#
	# What separates it from a reader is not the address and not the User-Agent:
	#
	#   * **No cookies.** 0 of 14,400 /app requests in that hour carried a
	#     `Cookie` header — not one.
	#   * **No assets.** 21 asset requests against 14,400 pages. A browser that
	#     rendered those pages would have asked for the stylesheet.
	#   * 9,726 of them sent `Accept: */*` and no `Sec-Fetch-*` headers at all.
	#
	# So the reader asks for a cookie. A request to /app without one gets this
	# page: `Set-Cookie`, an explanation, and one scripted reload, after which
	# the cookie is present and every later request is served normally. A browser
	# passes it once a month and sees a flash; an HTTP client with no cookie jar
	# gets 403 and no statutory text, at the proxy, without reaching Astro,
	# FastAPI or Postgres — which is the property that matters, since what the
	# scrape costs is a server-side render and four API calls per page.
	#
	# Deliberately not defended: a scraper that keeps cookies. The token is one
	# static value and copying it is one line of their code. This buys the
	# escalation rather than ending it, and the next rung — a proof-of-work
	# interstitial, or a CDN with a managed challenge — is a decision about what
	# this demo is for, recorded in ADR-0088 rather than pre-empted here.
	#
	# `/api/v1` is **not** gated. Programmatic use of the API is the point of the
	# API (ADR-0037 said so about the crawler list and it still holds); what
	# bounds it is ADR-0029's per-caller budgets and the global axis budget added
	# with this block.
	#
	# The gate is keyed on the hostname so it is off everywhere but the site it
	# was measured on: `USC_GATE_HOST` defaults to `gate.invalid`, which matches
	# no request, so `make dev-all`, the Playwright suite and `make shots` are
	# unaffected. The box sets it in `.env`; `deploy/watchdog.sh` sends the cookie,
	# or its probe of /app would read the gate as the site being down and restart
	# the containers every ten minutes.
	@gate {
		host {$USC_GATE_HOST:gate.invalid}
		path /app*
		not path /app/sw.js /app/manifest.webmanifest /app/offline /app/_astro/* /app/icons/* /app/fonts/*
		not header Cookie *{$USC_GATE_COOKIE:usc_h}=*
	}
	handle @gate {
		header Content-Type "text/html; charset=utf-8"
		# The gate page states the live state of one client and must not be
		# kept, least of all by the browser that is about to reload past it.
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		header Set-Cookie "{$USC_GATE_COOKIE:usc_h}={$USC_GATE_TOKEN:1}; Path=/; Max-Age=2592000; SameSite=Lax; Secure; HttpOnly"
		# One reload per browsing session, not a meta refresh: a browser with
		# cookies disabled would follow a refresh forever, and this asks once and
		# then explains itself. `script-src` carries 'unsafe-inline' (ADR-0030),
		# which is what lets this four-line script run under the site's own CSP.
		respond <<HTML
			<!doctype html>
			<html lang="en">
			<head>
			<meta charset="utf-8">
			<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
			<title>Checking your browser</title>
			<script>
			try {
			  if (!sessionStorage.getItem('usc-gate')) {
			    sessionStorage.setItem('usc-gate', '1');
			    location.reload();
			  }
			} catch (e) {}
			</script>
			</head>
			<body>
			<h1>Checking your browser</h1>
			<p>This page should reload itself. The reader needs cookies enabled; if
			you have them switched off, the pages under /app cannot be served.</p>
			<p>For automated access, use the API at
			<a href="/api/v1/status">/api/v1</a>, documented at
			<a href="/docs">/docs</a>. This site is closed to crawling — see
			<a href="/robots.txt">/robots.txt</a>.</p>
			</body>
			</html>
			HTML 403
	}

	# The transport timeouts are the proxy's half of ADR-0073's backpressure.
	# Without them Caddy waits indefinitely for a backend that has stopped
	# answering, so a wedged upstream becomes a growing pile of held client
	# connections here as well — the outage spreading to the one component that
	# was still working. `response_header_timeout` is generous enough for the
	# slowest legitimate render and short enough that a hang is reported as a
	# 502 rather than absorbed.
	handle /app* {
		reverse_proxy frontend:4321 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
			transport http {
				dial_timeout 5s
				response_header_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}

	# Everything else is FastAPI: /api/v1, the citation redirector at /us/usc,
	# /health, and the OpenAPI docs.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy api:8001 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
			transport http {
				dial_timeout 5s
				response_header_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}

	# Cache-Control is the origin's decision, never this file's: whether a
	# response may be kept forever depends on whether the release point was
	# pinned, which only the resolver knows (params.py, ADR-0018). Caddy passes
	# those headers through untouched and adds no cache of its own — it has no
	# built-in one, and the immutable headers are what a CDN in front would read
	# if one is ever added.
	# Browser security headers (ADR-0030). `?` sets a header only if the origin
	# did not, so a route can still speak for itself; none currently does.
	header {
		# Sent always; browsers are required to ignore it when it arrives over
		# plain HTTP (RFC 6797 §8.1), so the dev stack on :8000 is unaffected.
		#
		# `includeSubDomains` is a real commitment rather than a free upgrade:
		# every subdomain of $SITE_ADDRESS must then be HTTPS for as long as the
		# max-age, including ones that do not exist yet. Accepted because this is
		# a single-host deployment (ADR-0020), and a plain-HTTP sibling of a site
		# serving law is not something to leave room for.
		?Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		# The reader renders statutory XML into HTML; these cost nothing and
		# close the obvious own-goals.
		?X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		?Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

		# Nothing here has any reason to be framed, and being framed is how a
		# reader gets clickjacked into a watchlist change or a logout. Both
		# spellings: `frame-ancestors` below is the one that counts,
		# X-Frame-Options is for anything predating it.
		?X-Frame-Options "DENY"

		# The site loads no third-party anything — no CDN, no web font, no
		# analytics, which is downstream of ADR-0011's no-bundle choice. So
		# `default-src 'self'` is not a restriction that had to be negotiated,
		# it is a description of what the site already does, and that makes it
		# worth enforcing as the backstop under the reader's `set:html` sinks.
		#
		# `script-src` must carry 'unsafe-inline' for now, and saying so plainly
		# is better than implying the policy is tighter than it is: every island
		# is `<script is:inline>` — eight of them — which is ADR-0022's
		# deliberate no-framework choice, and the theme toggle in particular has
		# to run inline before first paint or the page flashes light before going
		# dark (ADR-0027). Nonces are the fix, and they need the Astro middleware
		# ADR-0029 has just added, so that is a follow-up with somewhere to go
		# rather than a wish. `style-src` is the same story: USWDS ships bundled
		# CSS, but a handful of components still carry `style="…"` attributes.
		#
		# `worker-src 'self' blob:` is here for exactly one thing: ReDoc builds
		# its search index in a worker created from a Blob, and with no
		# `worker-src` the browser falls back to `script-src`, which has no
		# `blob:` — so `/redoc` rendered in full and its search box silently
		# returned nothing for every query (measured, ADR-0032). Naming the
		# directive is what keeps the widening *narrow*: it permits a worker
		# from a blob and changes nothing about what may execute in the page,
		# which is the part `script-src` governs and which stays as it was.
		#
		# The reader itself has no worker of any kind, so this buys the reader
		# nothing and costs it nothing — it is a docs-page allowance that has to
		# live in a site-wide header because there is one header. The
		# alternative was leaving a documented page half-broken to keep a
		# directive absent, which is a worse trade than it sounds: an absent
		# directive is not a stricter one, it is `script-src` doing double duty.
		?Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'"
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
